<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Country</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rohe Pōtae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The King Country, in Māori Rohe Pōtae, is a region of the central North Island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> and Taranaki, home mainly to Ngāti Maniapoto. After the invasion of the Waikato in 1863–1864, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a> and his followers withdrew there, and its leaders kept Europeans out behind a boundary called the aukati. It was opened in the 1880s, when surveys for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-island-main-trunk">North Island Main Trunk railway</a> began.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/king-country.jpg" alt="King Country" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peetel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Country_location_in_New_Zealand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The King Country, also called the Western Uplands, reaches roughly from Kawhia Harbour and Ōtorohanga in the north to the upper Whanganui River in the south, and from the Hauhungaroa and Rangitoto Ranges in the east to near the Tasman Sea. It is rolling hill country, much of it forested, with extensive karst that has produced features such as the Waitomo Caves. Before European settlement it was home to several iwi, especially Ngāti Maniapoto, Ngāti Tama and Ngāti Tūwharetoa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-grey">Sir George Grey</a> ordered the invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> in July 1863, the outnumbered Kingite forces retreated south after the battle at Ōrākau in April 1864 onto Maniapoto land. The Māori name Rohe Pōtae, Area of the Hat, is said to come from the second Māori King, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a>, placing his top hat on a map of the North Island and declaring the land beneath it under his mana. The boundary, the aukati, was a line of pā along the Puniu River near Kihikihi. Some 3,500 Waikato people strained the resources of about 800 Maniapoto, and Te Kooti was granted asylum there on 15 May 1872.</p><p>In 1881 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhiao">Tāwhiao</a> laid down the King Movement's arms. Negotiations with Wahanui, Rewi and Taonui opened the district to Europeans by 1883, with alcohol prohibited throughout it. John Bryce obtained consent to survey the railway in March 1883, and at Kihikihi in February 1885 John Ballance won approval for its construction, which lasted from 1885 to 1908 and helped towns such as Taumarunui grow. King Country leaders petitioned again and again to keep the alcohol ban, which ended when a poll of November 1954 carried licensing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The King Country is no unit of local government: it lies within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikato">Waikato</a> and Manawatū-Whanganui regions and in all or part of the Ōtorohanga, Ruapehu, Taupō and Waitomo districts. It is rural and sparsely settled, its chief towns being Te Kūiti, Ōtorohanga and Taumarunui, and it lives mainly by pastoral farming and forestry. Pureora Forest Park is among its conservation lands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “King Country” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After their defeat at Ōrākau in 1864 they withdrew into the territory of Ngāti Maniapoto, which became known as the <b>King Country</b>, and Tāwhiao declared that Europeans risked death if they crossed the aukati, or boundary, of the confiscated land.</blockquote><a href="/movement/maori-king-movement">Māori King movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>King Tāwhiao withdrew south into the <b>Rohe Pōtae</b>, the King Country, which remained closed to Europeans behind a boundary called the aukati.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingitanga">Kingitanga</a></li></ul></section>
